    "en": {
      "title": "Ability and skill",
      "summary": "Can and could express ability; be able to fills the missing forms.",
      "status": "draft",
      "sections": [
        {
          "title": "What it is",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraphs",
              "paragraphs": [
                "Can and could express ability. Be able to is used where can has no form."
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "bullets",
              "items": [
                "I **can** swim.",
                "She **could** read at four.",
                "We **will be able to** come."
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "title": "How it works in English",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraphs",
              "paragraphs": [
                "Can has only two forms, can and could. For the future or the perfect, use be able to. Cannot is written as one word, and the short form is can't."
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "table",
              "html": "<table><thead><tr><th>Tense</th><th>Form</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>present</td><td><strong>can</strong></td><td><strong>I can drive.</strong></td></tr><tr><td>past</td><td><strong>could</strong></td><td><strong>He could ski.</strong></td></tr><tr><td>future</td><td><strong>will be able to</strong></td><td><strong>They will be able to help.</strong></td></tr></tbody></table>"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  }
